{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q019_stable_periodic_release_semantics","answer":"The current-release endpoint reports SCOPe 2.08 as the current stable release (stable_label "2.08-stable", freeze date 2021-07-28, public release date 2021-09-22, is_public true, is_comprehensive false, old_version null, api_url /api/v1/releases/2.08). The latest periodic update it reports is version "2.08-2026-08-16", dated 2026-08-16.

For a reproducible analysis, cite the fixed stable release identifier, SCOPe 2.08 (2.08-stable). The stable release is a frozen, versioned snapshot: its set of classified domains, sids, sunids, and sccs assignments does not change after the freeze, so anyone re-running the analysis against 2.08 gets the same classification. If you specifically depended on domains only present in an update, cite the exact dated periodic label (e.g. 2.08-2026-08-16) alongside the stable release, because "latest periodic update" is a moving target and the endpoint's answer will change the next time an update is published.

The periodic label means that SCOPe 2.08 is being incrementally extended between stable releases: new PDB entries are added automatically to existing families/superfamilies (and some manual curation), using the 2.08 classification hierarchy, and these additions are published as dated updates whose version string is the stable version plus the update date (2.08-YYYY-MM-DD). Periodic updates add newly classified structures but do not restructure the hierarchy; the stable label (2.08-stable) refers to the frozen baseline, while a dated periodic version refers to the baseline plus all automated additions through that date. Note that is_comprehensive is false, meaning 2.08 does not classify every PDB entry, so absence of a structure from the release (or from a given periodic update) is not evidence it lacks a classifiable domain.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases/current"]}]}
